(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112000-eighteen-of-twenty-harnesses-carry-the-table-layer-and-two-should-not")
  (:adr/topic "q9-harness-relevel")
  (:adr/title "Eighteen of twenty harnesses carry the table layer, and the two that do not should keep what they have")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609111900-predicting-what-a-repair-must-not-change-is-what-made-it-readable"))
  (:adr/body
  "## What was uneven

The entity-table layer was added mid-way through generating the cohort's
harnesses, so the ones produced before it compared four layers and the ones
after compared five. That showed up directly in the previous iteration's
verification counts -- 23 rows compared in some repositories and 29 in others --
which is how it was noticed.

Eleven harnesses lacked the layer, not the nine estimated last iteration. The
estimate was made from the row counts of a partial run rather than by asking
every repository.

## Nine were re-generated; two were deliberately left alone

Nine were generator output and simply predate the layer. Regenerated, compiled,
executed, verified: entity table agreeing in all nine.

`com-abb-robotics` and `com-accela` also lack the table layer, and were NOT
regenerated. Their harnesses are hand-written and carry MORE than the generator
emits -- store, filters, pagination, handlers, expand, and the float arm.
Replacing them with generator output would trade several layers of
repository-specific behaviour for one layer of table data.

That is worth stating as a decision rather than an oversight, because a
completeness count invites levelling everything to the same shape, and here the
uneven pair is the richer one. `18 of 20` is the honest figure and the two
exceptions are better than the eighteen.

## Why the layer was worth backfilling at all

Of the four layers those nine harnesses had, `page-limit` cannot vary across
this cohort: every pilot declares `default-limit 20` and `max-limit 100`. So
each of those harnesses carried one row of per-repository signal -- the as-int
control -- and three that could not distinguish its repository from any other.

The entity table changes that: it hashes
`entity|plural|id-prefix|fields|required|coerce` per spec from the repository's
own table on both sides.

## A detail that confirms it tracks the data

`com-adyen` and `com-aftership` produce FIVE table rows where the rest produce
six, because they declare five entities. The same two produce a route count of
25 where the rest produce 30. Two independently derived quantities moving
together with the entity table is what distinguishes a derivation from a
constant.

## Verification

Each of the nine: harness regenerated, component compiled to aarch64-macos,
artifact executed through `tools/kexe_loader.c`, entity table compared row by
row. Six of six for the six-entity repositories, five of five for the two
five-entity ones, zero disagreements.")))
